{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794507862,"nonce":"k6k7/lGCfYewGPX+PkDPaq2OhrM=","message":"Caribou, the only embedder, run against this checkout: a clone of caribou with all six ash crates patched to the local tree in the clone's .cargo/config.toml (a --config on the command line does not reach the nested cargo that its build script and program build start). Its wasm AOT tests, which pass links, exports, closures, tails, drops and fibers:\n\n- aot: passes\n- linked: 4 of 5 pass (wren module linked in, plugin crate linked in, plugin crate as a side module, plugin data both ways)\n- linked::futures_settle_in_a_threaded_wasm_program fails: \"could not reserve 536870912 bytes for the heap\" from ash's own heap in a wasm32-wasip1-threads module run by `caribou run`. It fails identically with ash at d6128244, where LLVM built the same program, so it is not from this work and I did not chase it. The shared memory the linker declares is 1 GB.\n\nCaribou found a real defect in the direct backend that no ash test can: the polls read the data symbol ash_fiber_poll_epoch, but a hosting runtime ticks an epoch of its own and exposes its address through hlp_fiber_poll_epoch_address (the LLVM wasm build always fetched it that way). Under Caribou the requested collections therefore waited until the heap was nearly full: with `ASH_GC_STATS=1` the first collection was origin=hard-pressure, where the LLVM build's were origin=poll every 8 MB, and the two plugin tests' `Vec2.live() \u003c 200000` check failed. Fixed in 1aa0c623, and wasm_target now asserts the object asks the runtime for the address and never names the bare symbol. After it, live() peaks at the same value as the LLVM build.\n\nCaribou's own pinned ash (e05d246d) does not build its runtime on this machine: two revs of ash_wasm_runtime collide in one archive (duplicate symbol ash_fiber_enter). That is on Caribou's side and was there before this work.","files":null}]}